window.CRS_PH_TOPICS = ["Big Picture & Cardiac Cell Types","Conduction System Anatomy","Fast vs Slow Response","Phase 0 & Na⁺ Channel Gates","Phase 1 · The Notch","Phase 2 · Plateau & E–C Coupling","Phases 3 & 4 · Reset and Rest","Refractory Periods","Na⁺ Channel States","Why the AP & Refractory Period Are Long","Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's"];
window.CRS_PH_QUESTIONS = [{"t":"Big Picture & Cardiac Cell Types","lv":"Easy","s":"According to the big picture of cardiac electrophysiology, the heart's electrical activity dictates which of the following?","c":["Its coronary blood flow pattern","Its heart valve structure","Its mechanical function as a pump","Its venous return to the atria","The layout of its fibrous skeleton"],"a":2,"e":"The heart's electrical activity dictates its mechanical function — the pump. It does two jobs: generate a rhythm and coordinate contraction.","w":["Coronary flow is not what the electrical system dictates.","Valves are passive structures, not set by electrical activity.","","Venous return is set mainly by the veins and the pressure gradient.","The fibrous skeleton is connective tissue, not set by electrical activity."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Big Picture & Cardiac Cell Types","lv":"Easy","s":"Which cells create the impulse that generates the heart's rhythm?","c":["Pacemaker cells","Ventricular myocytes","Atrial myocytes","Endothelial cells","Cardiac fibroblasts"],"a":0,"e":"Job 1 of the electrical system is to generate a rhythm: pacemaker cells create the impulse. Job 2 is to coordinate contraction through the conduction system.","w":["","Ventricular myocytes need an external stimulus; they have no automaticity.","Atrial myocytes are contractile workers, not pacemakers.","Endothelial cells line the chambers and play no part in the rhythm.","Fibroblasts make connective tissue; they do not fire impulses."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Big Picture & Cardiac Cell Types","lv":"Easy","s":"The cardiac conduction system spreads the impulse so that which sequence of contraction occurs?","c":["Ventricles contract before atria","Atria contract before ventricles","Atria and ventricles contract together","Right heart contracts before left","Apex contracts after the base"],"a":1,"e":"The conduction system spreads the impulse so atria contract before ventricles, giving the ventricles time to fill before they eject.","w":["This reverses the normal order; ventricles fill from the atria first.","","Simultaneous contraction would leave no time for atrial filling of the ventricles.","The two sides contract together; the key order is atria then ventricles.","Ventricular contraction actually spreads from apex toward base."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Big Picture & Cardiac Cell Types","lv":"Easy","s":"Antiarrhythmic drugs mainly act on which targets?","c":["Cardiac gap junctions","Myosin cross-bridges","Cardiac fibrous skeleton","Coronary endothelium","Specific ion channels"],"a":4,"e":"Arrhythmias are diseases of the electrical system. Antiarrhythmic drugs target the specific Na⁺, Ca²⁺ and K⁺ channels covered in this lecture.","w":["Gap junctions are not the stated target of antiarrhythmics.","Cross-bridges are contractile proteins, not the electrical system.","The fibrous skeleton is insulating connective tissue, not a drug target.","The endothelium is not where antiarrhythmics act.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Big Picture & Cardiac Cell Types","lv":"Easy","s":"Contractile myocytes, the \"workers\" of the heart, are found in which location?","c":["SA node and AV node only","Bundle of His and bundle branches","Purkinje fibers of the ventricles","Atrial and ventricular myocardium","The cardiac fibrous skeleton"],"a":3,"e":"Contractile myocytes sit in the atrial and ventricular myocardium. Their function is force production and contraction — mechanical work.","w":["These are pacemaker/conducting cells, the \"managers\".","These are conducting cells, not contractile workers.","Purkinje fibers are conducting cells, specialised for rapid conduction.","","The fibrous skeleton is connective tissue, not myocytes."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Big Picture & Cardiac Cell Types","lv":"Easy","s":"Which type of action potential do contractile cardiac myocytes produce?","c":["Slow response","Pacemaker potential","Graded potential","Plateau-free spike","Fast response"],"a":4,"e":"The electrical property of contractile myocytes is the \"fast response\" action potential, with a fast Na⁺ upstroke.","w":["Slow response APs belong to the SA and AV nodes.","A pacemaker potential is the spontaneous Phase 4 of nodal cells.","Myocytes fire an all-or-none AP, not a graded one.","The ventricular AP has a long plateau, not a brief spike.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Big Picture & Cardiac Cell Types","lv":"Easy","s":"Cardiac myocytes are connected to each other by gap junctions located within which structures?","c":["Transverse tubules","Sarcoplasmic reticulum","Z discs","Desmosome-free sarcolemma","Intercalated discs"],"a":4,"e":"Myocytes are connected by gap junctions within intercalated discs, forming a functional syncytium.","w":["T tubules carry the AP into the cell; they do not join neighbouring cells.","The SR stores Ca²⁺ inside the cell.","Z discs anchor actin within a sarcomere.","Gap junctions sit in intercalated discs, which also hold desmosomes.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Big Picture & Cardiac Cell Types","lv":"Easy","s":"Gap junctions allow the atrial or ventricular myocardium to contract as a single unit. This arrangement is called a what?","c":["Motor unit","Anatomical syncytium","Functional syncytium","Sarcomere","Neuromuscular junction"],"a":2,"e":"Gap junctions within intercalated discs form a functional syncytium, so the entire atrial or ventricular myocardium contracts as a single unit.","w":["A motor unit is a skeletal motor neuron plus its fibers.","Cardiac cells stay separate cells; they are functionally, not anatomically, fused.","","A sarcomere is the contractile unit inside one cell.","Cardiac myocytes are not activated through motor end plates."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Conduction System Anatomy","lv":"Easy","s":"Which of the following is a pacemaker/conducting cell location rather than contractile myocardium?","c":["Ventricular free wall","Interventricular septal myocardium","Atrial appendage","Bundle of His","Papillary muscle"],"a":3,"e":"Pacemaker/conducting cells (\"managers\") are located in the SA node, AV node, bundle of His and Purkinje fibers.","w":["The ventricular wall is contractile myocardium.","Septal muscle is contractile, though the bundle runs within the septum.","Atrial muscle is contractile myocardium.","","Papillary muscles are contractile ventricular muscle."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Conduction System Anatomy","lv":"Easy","s":"What is the defining key feature of pacemaker/conducting cells?","c":["A stable resting potential","Spontaneous depolarization","A long Phase 2 plateau","Large force production","A fast Na⁺ upstroke"],"a":1,"e":"Pacemaker/conducting cells have the ability to spontaneously depolarize. Their function is automaticity (self-excitation) and rapid impulse conduction.","w":["A stable rest is a feature of contractile myocytes.","","Nodal cells lack a plateau.","Force production is the job of contractile workers.","SA and AV nodal cells use a slow Ca²⁺ upstroke."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Conduction System Anatomy","lv":"Easy","s":"Where is the sinoatrial node located?","c":["Interatrial septum near the coronary sinus","Right atrial wall below the SVC opening","Interventricular septum","Left atrial wall near the pulmonary veins","Right ventricle near the tricuspid valve"],"a":1,"e":"The SA node lies in the right atrial wall, inferior to the opening of the superior vena cava.","w":["This describes the AV node.","","This is where the bundle of His runs.","The SA node is in the right atrium.","The SA node is atrial, not ventricular."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Conduction System Anatomy","lv":"Easy","s":"Where is the atrioventricular node located?","c":["Right atrial wall below the SVC opening","Interventricular septum below the fibrous skeleton","Subendocardium of both ventricles","Left atrial wall near the mitral valve","Interatrial septum near the coronary sinus"],"a":4,"e":"The AV node lies in the interatrial septum, anterior to the opening of the coronary sinus.","w":["This is the SA node.","This is the bundle of His.","This is where Purkinje fibers spread.","The AV node sits in the interatrial septum.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Conduction System Anatomy","lv":"Easy","s":"What is the only muscular connection between the atria and the ventricles?","c":["AV bundle of His","Internodal pathways","Right bundle branch","Purkinje fibers","Cardiac fibrous skeleton"],"a":0,"e":"The AV bundle of His, running in the interventricular septum, is the ONLY muscular connection between the atria and the ventricles; the fibrous skeleton insulates everywhere else.","w":["","Internodal pathways connect SA and AV nodes within the atria.","Bundle branches lie below the bundle, within the ventricles.","Purkinje fibers spread through the ventricles.","The fibrous skeleton is insulating connective tissue, not muscle."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Conduction System Anatomy","lv":"Easy","s":"Which structures carry the impulse from the SA node to the AV node?","c":["Internodal pathways","Left bundle branch","Purkinje fiber network","Bundle of His","Coronary sinus wall"],"a":0,"e":"The conduction order on the slide is: 1 SA node → 2 internodal pathways → 3 AV node → 4 AV bundle of His → 5a/5b left and right bundle branches → 6 Purkinje fibers.","w":["","The bundle branches lie below the bundle of His.","Purkinje fibers are ventricular.","The bundle lies after the AV node.","The coronary sinus is a vein, a landmark for the AV node."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Conduction System Anatomy","lv":"Easy","s":"Which structure is the last part of the conduction pathway before the ventricular muscle?","c":["AV node","Bundle of His","Purkinje fibers","Left bundle branch","Internodal pathways"],"a":2,"e":"SA node → internodal pathways → AV node → bundle of His → right and left bundle branches → Purkinje fibers, which spread through the ventricles.","w":["The AV node is early in the path, in the interatrial septum.","The bundle comes before the bundle branches.","","Bundle branches feed into the Purkinje fibers.","These are within the atria."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Fast vs Slow Response","lv":"Easy","s":"What is the resting membrane potential of a fast response ventricular myocyte?","c":["Unstable, with no true rest","Stable, about −60 mV","Stable, about −90 mV","Stable, about −40 mV","Stable, about −70 mV"],"a":2,"e":"Fast response (ventricular myocardium): stable resting potential ≈ −90 mV. Slow response (SA/AV node): unstable, no true resting potential.","w":["This describes slow response SA/AV nodal cells.","Too depolarized for a ventricular myocyte.","","This is close to the nodal threshold, not a ventricular rest.","−70 mV is a typical neuron value, not ventricular."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Fast vs Slow Response","lv":"Easy","s":"What current produces the Phase 0 upstroke of a slow response SA or AV nodal cell?","c":["Fast Na⁺ influx","Slow Ca²⁺ influx","Transient K⁺ efflux","Cl⁻ influx","K⁺ efflux via IK1"],"a":1,"e":"Slow response cells depolarize by slow Ca²⁺ influx through L-type Ca²⁺ channels. Memory hook: Fast = Na⁺ upstroke · Slow = Ca²⁺ upstroke.","w":["Fast Na⁺ influx drives the fast response upstroke.","","Transient K⁺ efflux causes the Phase 1 notch.","Cl⁻ is not the upstroke current in either cell type.","IK1 holds rest; it does not depolarize."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Fast vs Slow Response","lv":"Easy","s":"Which channel carries the Phase 0 upstroke current in ventricular myocardium?","c":["Voltage-gated Na⁺ channel","L-type Ca²⁺ channel","Inward-rectifier K⁺ channel","Delayed-rectifier K⁺ channel","Transient outward K⁺ channel"],"a":0,"e":"Fast response cells (ventricular myocardium) depolarize through fast Na⁺ influx via voltage-gated Na⁺ channels.","w":["","L-type channels carry the nodal upstroke and the plateau current.","IK1 holds the resting potential.","This repolarizes the cell in Phase 3.","This causes the Phase 1 notch."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Fast vs Slow Response","lv":"Easy","s":"Which cardiac cell type has a Phase 2 plateau?","c":["SA nodal cell","Ventricular myocyte","AV nodal cell","Both SA and AV nodal cells","None of the cardiac cells"],"a":1,"e":"Plateau (Phase 2): present in fast response ventricular myocardium, absent in slow response SA/AV nodal cells.","w":["Slow response cells have no plateau.","","Slow response cells have no plateau.","The plateau is absent in both nodal cell types.","Fast response ventricular cells do have a plateau."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Fast vs Slow Response","lv":"Easy","s":"Ventricular myocytes lack automaticity. What do they need in order to fire?","c":["A spontaneous Phase 4 drift","Sympathetic stimulation","An external stimulus","A falling extracellular K⁺","A fall in intracellular Ca²⁺"],"a":2,"e":"Automaticity is absent in fast response cells — they need an external stimulus. Slow response cells have automaticity, driven by Phase 4. Only the slow ones fire on their own.","w":["Only slow response nodal cells drift on their own.","Sympathetic input changes rate and force but is not required to fire.","","That is not what triggers a ventricular AP.","That is not the trigger for depolarization."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 0 & Na⁺ Channel Gates","lv":"Easy","s":"In which cardiac cells does the ventricular-type action potential occur?","c":["Only the ventricular myocytes","Only SA and AV nodal cells","Only the Purkinje fibers in the ventricles","Only the atrial myocytes and SA node","All myocytes except SA and AV nodes"],"a":4,"e":"The ventricular action potential occurs in all cardiac myocytes except for those in the SA and AV nodes.","w":["Atrial myocytes and Purkinje fibers also have this type of AP.","Nodal cells are the exception; they fire slow response APs.","It occurs in all cardiac myocytes, not only Purkinje fibers.","Atrial myocytes, yes, but the SA node is the exception.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 0 & Na⁺ Channel Gates","lv":"Easy","s":"Which ion movement causes the Phase 0 upstroke of the ventricular AP?","c":["K⁺ efflux","Ca²⁺ influx","Cl⁻ influx","Na⁺ influx","Na⁺ efflux"],"a":3,"e":"Phase 0: rapid upstroke and depolarization as voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open and Na⁺ rushes in (INa).","w":["K⁺ efflux repolarizes the cell.","Ca²⁺ influx sustains the plateau.","Cl⁻ is not the upstroke current.","","Na⁺ moves into the cell, down its gradient."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 0 & Na⁺ Channel Gates","lv":"Easy","s":"At about what membrane potential is threshold crossed to trigger the ventricular Phase 0 upstroke?","c":["−90 mV","−40 mV","0 mV","−65 mV","+30 mV"],"a":3,"e":"Phase 0: a neighbour depolarizes the cell past threshold (≈ −65 mV) → fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open → rapid Na⁺ influx → TMP rises to just above 0 mV.","w":["−90 mV is the resting potential.","−40 mV is closer to a nodal cell threshold.","0 mV is reached at the peak, not at threshold.","","+30 mV is around the overshoot peak."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 0 & Na⁺ Channel Gates","lv":"Easy","s":"In a ventricular myocyte, what normally depolarizes the cell to threshold?","c":["Its own spontaneous Phase 4 drift","The funny current in the cell","Ca²⁺ release from its own SR","A neighbouring depolarized cell","A Phase 3 burst of K⁺ efflux"],"a":3,"e":"A neighbour depolarizes the cell past threshold through gap junctions, opening fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels.","w":["Ventricular myocytes have no spontaneous Phase 4 depolarization.","The funny current is a nodal pacemaker current.","SR Ca²⁺ release follows the AP; it does not start it.","","K⁺ efflux repolarizes, it does not depolarize."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 0 & Na⁺ Channel Gates","lv":"Easy","s":"In the voltage-gated Na⁺ channel, the m gate is which type of gate?","c":["Inactivation gate","Ligand gate","Activation gate","Recovery gate","Selectivity filter"],"a":2,"e":"Na⁺ channel gates: M → activation gates; H → inactivation gates. At rest m is closed and h is open; in Phase 0 both are open.","w":["That is the h gate.","Not the m gate; these channels are voltage gated.","","Not a gate named in the lecture.","The filter is part of the pore, not a gate."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 0 & Na⁺ Channel Gates","lv":"Easy","s":"What property of the ventricular upstroke gives it fast conduction?","c":["A steep Na⁺ upstroke","A long Phase 2 plateau","A large Phase 3 K⁺ efflux","A slow Ca²⁺ upstroke","A Phase 4 drift"],"a":0,"e":"Steep Na⁺ upstroke → fast conduction. Nodal cells lack it, so they conduct slowly.","w":["","The plateau sets AP length, not conduction speed.","Phase 3 repolarizes; it does not set conduction speed.","A slow Ca²⁺ upstroke is why nodal cells conduct slowly.","Ventricular cells have no Phase 4 drift."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 1 · The Notch","lv":"Easy","s":"What causes Phase 1, the notch of the ventricular AP?","c":["Ca²⁺ channels open and all K⁺ channels close","Na⁺ channels inactivate, transient K⁺ opens","The Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pumps K⁺ back into the cell","Inward-rectifier K⁺ channels open fully again","The Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger reverses its direction"],"a":1,"e":"Phase 1: Na⁺ channels inactivate and transient K⁺ channels open (Ito) → a brief, partial repolarization toward 0 mV — the notch.","w":["This is not the notch mechanism.","","The pump restores gradients; it does not make the notch.","IK1 dominates Phase 4, not Phase 1.","The exchanger is not the cause of the notch."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 1 · The Notch","lv":"Easy","s":"Phase 1 brings the membrane potential toward approximately what value?","c":["~0 mV","−90 mV","−65 mV","+60 mV","−50 mV"],"a":0,"e":"Phase 1 is a brief, partial repolarization (the notch) from the peak toward ~0 mV.","w":["","−90 mV is the resting potential, reached in Phase 4.","−65 mV is the threshold for Phase 0.","The AP never reaches the Na⁺ equilibrium potential.","−50 mV is where the ARP ends, in Phase 3."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 1 · The Notch","lv":"Easy","s":"Which statement best describes the ionic event of Phase 1?","c":["Na⁺ rushes in","Transient K⁺ out","Ca²⁺ in ≈ K⁺ out","K⁺ out > Ca²⁺ in","IK1 holds −90 mV"],"a":1,"e":"Summary: 0 Na⁺ rushes in — upstroke; 1 transient K⁺ out — the notch; 2 Ca²⁺ in ≈ K⁺ out — plateau; 3 K⁺ out > Ca²⁺ in — repolarization; 4 IK1 holds −90 mV — rest.","w":["That is Phase 0.","","That is Phase 2.","That is Phase 3.","That is Phase 4."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 2 · Plateau & E–C Coupling","lv":"Easy","s":"How long does the Phase 2 plateau last?","c":["150–300 ms","1–2 ms","10–20 ms","500–800 ms","50–80 ms"],"a":0,"e":"Phase 2, the plateau, lasts 150–300 ms with TMP held near 0 mV.","w":["","This is the length of a whole neuron AP.","This is about a skeletal muscle AP.","Longer than the plateau.","Too short for the cardiac plateau."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 2 · Plateau & E–C Coupling","lv":"Easy","s":"Through which channels does Ca²⁺ enter the ventricular myocyte during the plateau?","c":["T-type Ca²⁺ channels","L-type Ca²⁺ channels","Ryanodine receptors","Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels","Store-operated Ca²⁺ channels"],"a":1,"e":"Phase 2: L-type Ca²⁺ channels open → Ca²⁺ influx, which is balanced by K⁺ efflux.","w":["T-type channels are not the plateau channel named.","","Ryanodine receptors release Ca²⁺ from the SR, not from outside.","These are inactivated during the plateau.","Not the plateau channel."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 2 · Plateau & E–C Coupling","lv":"Easy","s":"During the Phase 2 plateau, Ca²⁺ influx is balanced by which ion movement?","c":["Na⁺ influx","Cl⁻ efflux","Ca²⁺ efflux","K⁺ influx","K⁺ efflux"],"a":4,"e":"Plateau: Ca²⁺ in ≈ K⁺ out, holding the transmembrane potential near 0 mV.","w":["Fast Na⁺ channels are inactivated during the plateau.","Cl⁻ is not the balancing current named.","Ca²⁺ is moving in during Phase 2.","K⁺ moves out of the cell, not in.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 2 · Plateau & E–C Coupling","lv":"Easy","s":"What does the Ca²⁺ that enters during the plateau trigger inside the myocyte?","c":["Ca²⁺ release from the SR","Na⁺ channel recovery","Opening of IK1 channels","Phase 4 depolarization","Closure of gap junctions"],"a":0,"e":"Ca²⁺ influx through L-type channels triggers Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, which leads to myocyte contraction.","w":["","Na⁺ channels recover in late Phase 3.","IK1 is not triggered by Ca²⁺ entry.","Ventricular myocytes have no Phase 4 depolarization.","Not the effect of plateau Ca²⁺."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 2 · Plateau & E–C Coupling","lv":"Easy","s":"What is the correct order of events beginning with the plateau?","c":["SR Ca²⁺ release → Ca²⁺ influx → contraction","Contraction → Ca²⁺ influx → SR Ca²⁺ release","Ca²⁺ influx → contraction → SR Ca²⁺ release","Na⁺ influx → contraction → Ca²⁺ influx","Ca²⁺ influx → SR Ca²⁺ release → contraction"],"a":4,"e":"1) L-type Ca²⁺ channels open → Ca²⁺ influx; 2) Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release from the SR; 3) myocyte contraction.","w":["Influx from outside comes first and triggers SR release.","Contraction is the last step.","SR release has to come before contraction.","Contraction requires Ca²⁺ first.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 2 · Plateau & E–C Coupling","lv":"Easy","s":"For boards: what do cardiac myocytes require that skeletal muscle does not, in order to release SR Ca²⁺?","c":["Intracellular Na⁺","ATP from glycolysis","Acetylcholine at a motor end plate","Extracellular Ca²⁺","Extracellular K⁺"],"a":3,"e":"Cardiac myocytes REQUIRE extracellular Ca²⁺ to release SR Ca²⁺ — skeletal muscle does not.","w":["Na⁺ is not the trigger for SR release.","Not the distinguishing requirement.","That is how skeletal muscle is activated.","","K⁺ does not trigger SR release."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phases 3 & 4 · Reset and Rest","lv":"Easy","s":"What is the net current in Phase 3 of the ventricular AP?","c":["Net inward, Ca²⁺ > K⁺","Net outward, K⁺ > Ca²⁺","Zero net current, Ca²⁺ = K⁺","Net inward Na⁺ current","Net outward Cl⁻ current"],"a":1,"e":"Phase 3: K⁺ > Ca²⁺ — a net outward current brings the cell back to −90 mV.","w":["Inward Ca²⁺ is falling as the channels close.","","A zero net current is the plateau balance.","Na⁺ channels are inactivated or recovering.","Not the Phase 3 current."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phases 3 & 4 · Reset and Rest","lv":"Easy","s":"Which K⁺ channels produce the massive K⁺ efflux of Phase 3?","c":["Voltage-gated slow K⁺ channels","Transient outward K⁺ channels","ATP-sensitive K⁺ channels","Ca²⁺-activated K⁺ channels","Inward-rectifier K1 channels only"],"a":0,"e":"Phase 3: massive K⁺ efflux due to opening of voltage-gated slow (delayed-rectifier) K⁺ channels and closure of voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels.","w":["","These open briefly in Phase 1.","Not the channel named for Phase 3.","Not the channel named for Phase 3.","IK1 holds rest in Phase 4; the slow K⁺ channels drive Phase 3."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phases 3 & 4 · Reset and Rest","lv":"Easy","s":"Which transporters restore ionic gradients during and after Phase 3?","c":["Gap junctions and their connexins","L-type channels and ryanodine receptors","Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger and ATPases","Fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels","Inward-rectifier K⁺ channels"],"a":2,"e":"Phase 3: Ca²⁺ channels inactivate; sustained K⁺ efflux exceeds Ca²⁺ influx. The Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger and ATPases restore ionic gradients.","w":["These connect cells; they do not restore gradients.","These let Ca²⁺ move down gradients; they do not restore them.","","Channels let ions move down gradients.","IK1 holds rest; it does not restore gradients."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phases 3 & 4 · Reset and Rest","lv":"Easy","s":"Which channel holds the resting membrane potential of the ventricular myocyte?","c":["Voltage-gated Na⁺ channel","L-type Ca²⁺ channel","Inward-rectifier K⁺ channel","Transient outward K⁺ channel","HCN funny channel"],"a":2,"e":"Phase 4: constant K⁺ efflux through inward-rectifier channels (IK1) holds the RMP; Na⁺ and Ca²⁺ channels are closed.","w":["Na⁺ channels are closed at rest.","Ca²⁺ channels are closed at rest.","","Ito acts in Phase 1.","Funny channels are nodal pacemaker channels."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phases 3 & 4 · Reset and Rest","lv":"Easy","s":"Which property explains why the Phase 4 resting potential is close to the K⁺ equilibrium potential?","c":["High Na⁺ permeability","High Ca²⁺ permeability","High Cl⁻ permeability","Low K⁺ permeability","High K⁺ permeability"],"a":4,"e":"Phase 4: resting potential with high K⁺ permeability through K⁺ channels (IK1).","w":["High Na⁺ permeability would pull the cell toward +60 mV.","Ca²⁺ channels are closed at rest.","Not the dominant permeability at rest.","K⁺ permeability is high at rest.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phases 3 & 4 · Reset and Rest","lv":"Easy","s":"What happens in Phase 4 of a ventricular myocyte between beats?","c":["It drifts upward toward threshold","It waits at a stable −90 mV","It fires spontaneous APs","It stays near 0 mV","It hyperpolarizes to −120 mV"],"a":1,"e":"No spontaneous Phase 4 depolarization → myocytes wait for an external stimulus at a stable −90 mV.","w":["There is no spontaneous Phase 4 depolarization in myocytes.","","Myocytes lack automaticity.","0 mV is the plateau.","Not described; rest is about −90 mV."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Refractory Periods","lv":"Easy","s":"Which phases correspond to the absolute refractory period?","c":["Phases 0 and 1 only","Phase 4 and the start of Phase 0","Phases 0, 1, 2 and half of 3","Only the second half of Phase 3","All of Phases 3 and 4"],"a":2,"e":"Absolute refractory period: Phases 0, 1, 2 and half of Phase 3. Relative refractory period: second half of Phase 3. Then a very short supranormal period.","w":["The ARP lasts through the plateau and into Phase 3.","Phase 4 is rest, when the cell is excitable.","","This is the relative refractory period.","Too late; the ARP starts with Phase 0."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Refractory Periods","lv":"Easy","s":"Which part of the AP corresponds to the relative refractory period?","c":["The Phase 2 plateau","The Phase 1 notch","The whole of Phase 4 at rest","The Phase 0 upstroke","Second half of Phase 3"],"a":4,"e":"Relative refractory period = second half of Phase 3.","w":["Phase 2 is within the absolute refractory period.","Phase 1 is within the absolute refractory period.","At rest the cell is fully excitable.","Phase 0 is within the absolute refractory period.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Refractory Periods","lv":"Easy","s":"How long is the supranormal excitability period?","c":["As long as the plateau","All of Phase 4","All of Phase 3","Very short","About 300 ms"],"a":3,"e":"Supranormal excitability period: very short — a brief window of heightened excitability during repolarization.","w":["Much shorter than the plateau.","It is a brief window at the end of repolarization.","Phase 3 holds the ARP end and the RRP.","","That is the length of the whole AP."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Refractory Periods","lv":"Easy","s":"Why can't cardiac muscle generate tetanic contractions?","c":["Its lack of gap junctions","Its lack of SR","Its short action potential","Its fast Na⁺ upstroke","Its long refractory period"],"a":4,"e":"Due to long refractory periods, cardiac muscle cannot be re-excited during contraction — the inability to generate tetanic contractions. Heart tetany would be fatal.","w":["Myocytes have gap junctions.","Cardiac myocytes have an SR.","The cardiac AP is long, not short.","The upstroke does not prevent tetanus.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Refractory Periods","lv":"Easy","s":"During the absolute refractory period, what is the state of the fast Na⁺ channels?","c":["Resting and available","Open and activated","Fully recovered","Permanently blocked","Inactivated"],"a":4,"e":"ARP: Phases 0–2 and part of 3. Fast Na⁺ channels are inactivated → no new AP, whatever the stimulus.","w":["Resting channels could fire a new AP.","They are open only during Phase 0.","Recovery happens in late Phase 3.","They recover with repolarization.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Na⁺ Channel States","lv":"Easy","s":"In Phase 4, what is the state of the fast Na⁺ channel?","c":["Open and activated","Closed and inactivated","Recovering from inactivation","Resting and available","Permanently blocked"],"a":3,"e":"Phase 4: resting/available — closed, but able to open when stimulated (m closed · h open).","w":["That is Phase 0.","That is Phases 1–2.","That is late Phase 3.","","Not a normal channel state."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Na⁺ Channel States","lv":"Easy","s":"What are the positions of the m and h gates of the Na⁺ channel in Phase 0?","c":["m closed, h open","m open, h closed","m closed, h closed","m open, h open","Only the h gate open"],"a":3,"e":"Phase 0: open/activated — m open · h open → rapid Na⁺ influx.","w":["That is the resting state.","That is the inactivated state.","Not a listed state.","","Both gates are open in Phase 0."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Na⁺ Channel States","lv":"Easy","s":"Which describes the Na⁺ channel during Phases 1–2?","c":["Resting, m closed and h open","Inactivated, m open and h closed","Activated, m open and h open","Recovering, with the h gate reopening","Resting, m closed and h closed"],"a":1,"e":"Phases 1–2: inactivated — closed and unavailable, regardless of stimulus strength (m open · h closed).","w":["That is Phase 4.","","That is Phase 0.","That is late Phase 3.","Not a listed state; inactivation leaves m open."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Na⁺ Channel States","lv":"Easy","s":"What happens to Na⁺ channels during late Phase 3?","c":["The h gate reopens","The m gate opens","The h gate closes","They open for Na⁺ influx","They stay inactivated"],"a":0,"e":"Late Phase 3: recovering — channels progressively return to resting/available as repolarization completes (h gate reopening).","w":["","m opens in Phase 0.","h closes to inactivate in Phase 1.","Influx happens in Phase 0.","They progressively recover in late Phase 3."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Why the AP & Refractory Period Are Long","lv":"Easy","s":"About how long is a cardiac muscle action potential?","c":["300 ms","1 ms","10 ms","30 ms","3,000 ms"],"a":0,"e":"Approximate AP durations: neuron 1 ms, skeletal muscle 10 ms, cardiac muscle 300 ms.","w":["","This is a neuron AP.","This is a skeletal muscle AP.","Too short for a cardiac AP.","Too long for a cardiac AP."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Why the AP & Refractory Period Are Long","lv":"Easy","s":"The cardiac AP is about how many times longer than a skeletal muscle or nerve AP?","c":["2–3×","5–10×","30–300×","1,000–3,000×","Equal in length"],"a":2,"e":"The cardiac AP is 30–300× longer than a skeletal muscle (10 ms) or nerve (1 ms) AP.","w":["Far too small a difference.","Too small; even skeletal muscle is 30× shorter.","","Too large a difference.","The cardiac AP is much longer."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Why the AP & Refractory Period Are Long","lv":"Easy","s":"Which single phase stretches the cardiac AP to about 300 ms?","c":["Phase 0","Phase 1","Phase 2","Phase 3","Phase 4"],"a":2,"e":"The Phase 2 plateau — L-type Ca²⁺ influx balanced by delayed-rectifier K⁺ efflux, holding TMP near 0 mV — is the single phase that stretches the AP to ~300 ms.","w":["Phase 0 is a rapid upstroke.","Phase 1 is a brief notch.","","Phase 3 is rapid repolarization.","Phase 4 is rest, between APs."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Why the AP & Refractory Period Are Long","lv":"Easy","s":"Which benefit in diastole comes from the long plateau and long refractory period?","c":["Faster AV nodal conduction","A higher maximal heart rate","Sustained tetanic contraction","Time for ventricular filling","A shorter relaxation phase"],"a":3,"e":"Diastole: guaranteed relaxation → adequate time for ventricular filling.","w":["The refractory period does not speed AV conduction.","A long refractory period limits rate, not raises it.","The long refractory period prevents tetanus.","","Relaxation is guaranteed, not shortened."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Why the AP & Refractory Period Are Long","lv":"Easy","s":"Which benefit in systole comes from the long refractory period?","c":["Full ejection before the next impulse","Re-excitation partway through contraction","Faster ventricular filling in systole","A stronger fast Na⁺ current each beat","A continuous tetanic squeeze"],"a":0,"e":"Systole: contraction runs to completion → full ejection before the next impulse.","w":["","The refractory period prevents re-excitation.","Filling is a diastolic benefit.","Not a systolic benefit of refractoriness.","Tetanus is prevented, and would be fatal."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Why the AP & Refractory Period Are Long","lv":"Easy","s":"What is the flip side of having refractory periods that vary between regions?","c":["Heart tetany","Loss of automaticity","Skeletal muscle cramps","Re-entrant arrhythmias","Absent plateau"],"a":3,"e":"Flip side: heterogeneous refractory periods make re-entrant arrhythmias possible when an impulse arrives at the wrong time.","w":["Tetany is prevented by the long refractory period.","Not the flip side described.","Not related to cardiac refractory periods.","","The plateau is present."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Big Picture & Cardiac Cell Types","lv":"Hard","s":"A cell with a fast response action potential whose main role is force production would be found where?","c":["SA node in the right atrium","AV node in the interatrial septum","Internodal pathway","Left ventricular free wall","Bundle of His in the septum"],"a":3,"e":"Workers (contractile myocytes) = atrial and ventricular myocardium, fast response AP, force production. Managers = SA node, AV node, His, Purkinje.","w":["SA nodal cells are slow response managers.","AV nodal cells are slow response managers.","These are conducting cells, not force producers.","","The bundle conducts; it is not for force."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Big Picture & Cardiac Cell Types","lv":"Hard","s":"A drug that closes all gap junctions in the ventricles would most directly impair which property?","c":["Phase 0 Na⁺ influx in each cell","SR Ca²⁺ storage","The resting potential of each cell","Spontaneous SA node firing","Contraction as a single unit"],"a":4,"e":"Gap junctions in intercalated discs form a functional syncytium so the myocardium contracts as a single unit. Without them, the impulse cannot spread cell to cell.","w":["Each cell's Na⁺ channels still work.","SR storage does not depend on gap junctions.","IK1 still holds rest in each cell.","SA node automaticity does not depend on ventricular gap junctions.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Conduction System Anatomy","lv":"Hard","s":"If the AV bundle of His were cut, why could the atrial impulse not reach the ventricles by another muscular path?","c":["The internodal pathways end at the SA node","Purkinje fibers conduct only backward","The fibrous skeleton insulates the rest","The bundle branches are in the atria","Ventricular myocytes lack gap junctions"],"a":2,"e":"The AV bundle is the ONLY muscular connection between atria and ventricles; the cardiac fibrous skeleton separates them everywhere else.","w":["They run from the SA node to the AV node.","Not the reason.","","Bundle branches are ventricular.","Ventricular myocytes do have gap junctions."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Conduction System Anatomy","lv":"Hard","s":"During surgery near the opening of the coronary sinus in the interatrial septum, which conducting structure is most at risk?","c":["SA node","Bundle of His","Right bundle branch","AV node","Purkinje fibers"],"a":3,"e":"AV node: interatrial septum, anterior to the opening of the coronary sinus. SA node: right atrial wall, inferior to the SVC opening.","w":["The SA node is near the SVC opening.","The bundle is in the interventricular septum.","This lies in the ventricles.","","These are ventricular."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Fast vs Slow Response","lv":"Hard","s":"A cell has an unstable resting potential, no plateau, and an upstroke carried by L-type channels. Which statement is true of it?","c":["It fires without an external stimulus","It is a ventricular contractile myocyte","Its upstroke uses fast Na⁺ channels","It rests stably at −90 mV","It conducts the impulse fastest"],"a":0,"e":"This is a slow response SA/AV nodal cell: unstable rest, slow Ca²⁺ upstroke, no plateau, automaticity driven by Phase 4. Only the slow ones fire on their own.","w":["","Ventricular cells have a stable rest and a plateau.","Its upstroke is Ca²⁺ carried.","It has no true resting potential.","Slow upstroke means slow conduction."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Fast vs Slow Response","lv":"Hard","s":"Why does the impulse spread more slowly through the AV node than through ventricular muscle?","c":["Nodal cells have a longer Phase 2 plateau","Nodal cells rest stably at −90 mV","Nodal cells have more gap junctions","Nodal cells depolarize with fast Na⁺ channels","Nodal cells lack a steep Na⁺ upstroke"],"a":4,"e":"Steep Na⁺ upstroke → fast conduction. Nodal cells lack it (slow Ca²⁺ upstroke), so they conduct slowly.","w":["Nodal cells have no plateau.","They have no stable rest.","More gap junctions would speed conduction.","They use slow L-type Ca²⁺ channels.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 0 & Na⁺ Channel Gates","lv":"Hard","s":"Which sequence describes ventricular Phase 0 in order?","c":["Na⁺ influx → threshold → Na⁺ channels open → TMP above 0","Na⁺ channels open → threshold → TMP above 0 → Na⁺ influx","Threshold → Na⁺ channels open → Na⁺ influx → TMP above 0","Threshold → K⁺ channels open → Na⁺ influx → TMP above 0","Ca²⁺ influx → threshold → Na⁺ influx → TMP above 0"],"a":2,"e":"A neighbour depolarizes the cell past threshold → fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open → rapid Na⁺ influx → TMP rises to just above 0 mV.","w":["Channels must open before Na⁺ rushes in.","Threshold comes first.","","K⁺ channels open in Phase 1.","Ca²⁺ influx is the plateau current."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 0 & Na⁺ Channel Gates","lv":"Hard","s":"At the start of Phase 0, a ventricular Na⁺ channel goes from resting to open. Which gate changes position?","c":["Only the h gate opens","Both gates open from closed","The h gate closes","Both gates close","Only the m gate opens"],"a":4,"e":"Resting: m closed · h open. Open: m open · h open. So only the m (activation) gate changes when the channel opens.","w":["h is already open at rest.","h is already open at rest.","h closes later, in Phase 1.","Both are open in Phase 0.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 0 & Na⁺ Channel Gates","lv":"Hard","s":"On the ventricular AP, the overshoot is the portion of the AP that does what?","c":["Falls below −90 mV","Rises above 0 mV","Stays at the threshold","Plateaus near −40 mV","Repolarizes in Phase 3"],"a":1,"e":"Phase 0 drives the TMP to just above 0 mV, peaking near +30 mV; the part above 0 is the overshoot.","w":["That would be an afterhyperpolarization.","","Threshold is about −65 mV.","The plateau is near 0 mV.","Phase 3 is repolarization."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 1 · The Notch","lv":"Hard","s":"A drug blocks the transient outward K⁺ current. Which part of the ventricular AP would be most directly reduced?","c":["The Phase 0 upstroke","The Phase 2 Ca²⁺ influx","The Phase 1 notch","The Phase 4 rest","The Phase 3 delayed-rectifier efflux"],"a":2,"e":"Phase 1, the notch, is made by Na⁺ channel inactivation plus transient K⁺ channels opening (Ito). Blocking Ito blunts the notch.","w":["The upstroke is a Na⁺ current.","The plateau Ca²⁺ is through L-type channels.","","IK1, not Ito, holds the rest.","That is the slow K⁺ current."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 2 · Plateau & E–C Coupling","lv":"Hard","s":"Why does the plateau keep the TMP near 0 mV for 150–300 ms?","c":["All the ion channels are closed","Na⁺ influx is still high through fast channels","The Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase stops pumping","Ca²⁺ influx roughly equals K⁺ efflux","Gap junctions close between the cells"],"a":3,"e":"Plateau: L-type Ca²⁺ influx is balanced by delayed-rectifier K⁺ efflux — Ca²⁺ in ≈ K⁺ out — holding TMP near 0 mV.","w":["Channels are open; the currents balance.","Na⁺ channels are inactivated.","The pump does not cause the plateau.","","Gap junctions do not hold the plateau."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 2 · Plateau & E–C Coupling","lv":"Hard","s":"The lecture describes Phase 2 as \"not just an electrical plateau\". What else is it?","c":["The pacemaker potential","The relative refractory period","The Na⁺ recovery phase","The resting state","The contraction trigger"],"a":4,"e":"Key focus: Phase 2 Ca²⁺ influx links directly to SR Ca²⁺ release (excitation–contraction coupling) — it is not just an electrical plateau, it is the contraction trigger.","w":["The pacemaker potential is Phase 4 in nodal cells.","Phase 2 is within the absolute refractory period.","Na⁺ channels recover in late Phase 3.","Rest is Phase 4.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 2 · Plateau & E–C Coupling","lv":"Hard","s":"A heart is perfused with a solution containing no Ca²⁺. Skeletal muscle in the same bath still contracts. Why does the heart stop contracting?","c":["No Ca²⁺ influx triggers SR release","Fast Na⁺ channels cannot open","The SR has no Ca²⁺ stored in it","IK1 holds the cell near 0 mV instead","Gap junctions need Ca²⁺ to open"],"a":0,"e":"Cardiac myocytes REQUIRE extracellular Ca²⁺ influx through L-type channels to trigger Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release from the SR. Skeletal muscle does not.","w":["","Na⁺ channels do not need extracellular Ca²⁺ to open.","The SR still has stores; it is not triggered to release them.","IK1 holds −90 mV.","Not the mechanism given."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phase 2 · Plateau & E–C Coupling","lv":"Hard","s":"An L-type Ca²⁺ channel blocker is given. Which change in the ventricular myocyte is expected?","c":["A faster Phase 0 upstroke","Weaker contraction","A higher resting potential","A longer Phase 2 plateau","Spontaneous Phase 4 firing"],"a":1,"e":"Less Ca²⁺ influx through L-type channels → less Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release from the SR → weaker myocyte contraction.","w":["Phase 0 is a Na⁺ current.","","Rest is held by IK1.","Less inward Ca²⁺ would shorten, not lengthen, the plateau.","Ventricular cells do not gain automaticity."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phases 3 & 4 · Reset and Rest","lv":"Hard","s":"In Phase 3, why does the membrane repolarize?","c":["Na⁺ channels reopen and let Na⁺ out","The Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase stops working","Transient outward K⁺ channels reopen","Ca²⁺ channels close; K⁺ efflux goes on","Ca²⁺ influx grows larger than K⁺ efflux"],"a":3,"e":"Phase 3: Ca²⁺ channels inactivate; sustained K⁺ efflux exceeds Ca²⁺ influx → a net outward current returns the cell to −90 mV.","w":["Na⁺ moves in, not out, and those channels are not open.","The pump keeps running and helps restore gradients.","Ito acts in Phase 1.","","That would depolarize, not repolarize."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phases 3 & 4 · Reset and Rest","lv":"Hard","s":"Match the phase to its main current: which pairing is correct?","c":["Phase 4 · IK1","Phase 0 · ICa","Phase 1 · IK1","Phase 2 · INa","Phase 3 · ICa"],"a":0,"e":"Phase 0 · INa; Phase 1 · Ito/IK; Phase 2 · ICa and IK; Phase 3 · IK; Phase 4 · IK1.","w":["","Phase 0 of the ventricular AP is INa.","Phase 1 is Ito.","Phase 2 is ICa balanced by IK.","Phase 3 is IK."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Phases 3 & 4 · Reset and Rest","lv":"Hard","s":"A ventricular myocyte at rest is not stimulated. Why does it not fire?","c":["Its Na⁺ channels are all inactivated","There is no Phase 4 depolarization","Its threshold sits at −90 mV","Its IK1 channels are closed at rest","Its resting potential is unstable"],"a":1,"e":"No spontaneous Phase 4 depolarization → myocytes wait for an external stimulus.","w":["At rest they are available.","","Threshold is about −65 mV.","IK1 is open at rest.","Its rest is stable."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Refractory Periods","lv":"Hard","s":"A stimulus arrives during the Phase 2 plateau. What happens?","c":["A normal AP fires","A smaller AP fires at once","An AP fires if the stimulus is strong","A larger-than-normal AP fires","No new AP, whatever its strength"],"a":4,"e":"The plateau is within the absolute (effective) refractory period. Fast Na⁺ channels are inactivated → no new AP, whatever the stimulus.","w":["Na⁺ channels are inactivated.","That happens in the relative refractory period.","Even strong stimuli fail during the ARP.","That is not possible during the ARP.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Refractory Periods","lv":"Hard","s":"A stronger-than-normal stimulus arrives in late Phase 3. What is the result?","c":["No AP of any size can fire","A normal full-size AP","A new AP of lower amplitude","An AP larger than normal","A sustained tetanic contraction"],"a":2,"e":"Relative refractory period (second half of Phase 3): some Na⁺ channels have recovered → a stronger-than-normal stimulus is needed, and the AP has lower amplitude.","w":["Some Na⁺ channels have recovered.","Not all channels have recovered.","","Fewer available channels give a smaller AP.","Cardiac muscle cannot tetanize."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Refractory Periods","lv":"Hard","s":"On the refractory period figure, roughly what voltage marks the end of the absolute refractory period?","c":["−50 mV","−90 mV","−70 mV","0 mV","+20 mV"],"a":0,"e":"On the figure, the ARP ends around −50 mV in Phase 3, the RRP runs to about −70 mV, and the supranormal period follows.","w":["","−90 mV is rest.","−70 mV is about where the RRP ends.","0 mV is the plateau.","+20 mV is near the peak."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Refractory Periods","lv":"Hard","s":"During which period can a slightly weaker-than-normal stimulus fire an AP?","c":["Absolute refractory period","Relative refractory period","Supranormal period","The plateau","Phase 0"],"a":2,"e":"Supranormal: a brief window of heightened excitability during repolarization, so a weaker stimulus can fire.","w":["No stimulus fires an AP then.","A stronger stimulus is needed then.","","The plateau is in the ARP.","The cell is already firing."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Na⁺ Channel States","lv":"Hard","s":"The lecture states that excitability depends on Na⁺ channel state, not just on membrane voltage. Which example shows this?","c":["Inactivated channels fail at any stimulus","At rest the cell holds a stable potential","Plateau Ca²⁺ influx is balanced by K⁺ efflux","The cardiac AP is longer than a nerve AP","Gap junctions link neighbouring myocytes"],"a":0,"e":"Phases 1–2: channels are inactivated — closed and unavailable, regardless of stimulus strength. Excitability depends on Na⁺ channel state, not just voltage.","w":["","This does not involve channel state.","That describes the plateau, not excitability.","This is about duration.","Not about channel state."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Na⁺ Channel States","lv":"Hard","s":"Both the Phase 4 and the Phases 1–2 Na⁺ channel are closed to Na⁺ flow. What differs?","c":["The membrane is at the same voltage","Which gate is closed","Both m and h are open in each","Neither can ever open again","Both are in late Phase 3"],"a":1,"e":"Resting: m closed · h open — able to open. Inactivated: m open · h closed — unavailable. The closed gate decides whether the channel can respond.","w":["The voltages differ: −90 mV vs near 0 mV.","","Each has one gate closed.","The resting channel can open.","These are Phase 4 and Phases 1–2."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Na⁺ Channel States","lv":"Hard","s":"Why is the AP fired in the relative refractory period smaller than normal?","c":["All the K⁺ channels are closed","The L-type Ca²⁺ channels are inactivated","The m gates are all stuck shut","Only some Na⁺ channels have recovered","Extracellular Na⁺ has been depleted"],"a":3,"e":"Late Phase 3: channels progressively return to resting/available (h gate reopening). Fewer available channels → smaller Na⁺ current → lower-amplitude AP.","w":["K⁺ channels are open during Phase 3.","Ca²⁺ is not the upstroke current.","The recovering channels can open their m gates.","","The Na⁺ gradient is intact."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Why the AP & Refractory Period Are Long","lv":"Hard","s":"Which two currents together set the length of the cardiac plateau?","c":["Fast Na⁺ and transient outward K⁺","L-type Ca²⁺ and delayed-rectifier K⁺","Inward-rectifier K⁺ and the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase","Fast Na⁺ and L-type Ca²⁺","Funny current and T-type Ca²⁺"],"a":1,"e":"L-type Ca²⁺ influx is balanced by delayed-rectifier K⁺ efflux, holding TMP near 0 mV — the single phase that stretches the AP to ~300 ms.","w":["These are Phases 0 and 1.","","IK1 holds rest.","Fast Na⁺ channels are inactivated in Phase 2.","These are nodal pacemaker currents."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Why the AP & Refractory Period Are Long","lv":"Hard","s":"Which chain correctly links the long cardiac AP to its protective effect?","c":["Long refractory → no re-excitation → no tetany","Long refractory → re-excitation → tetany","Short refractory → no re-excitation → no tetany","Long plateau → faster heart rate → tetany","Short plateau → full ejection → no tetany"],"a":0,"e":"Long plateau + long refractory period → no re-excitation mid-contraction → no tetanic contraction.","w":["","Re-excitation is prevented.","The refractory period is long.","Tetany is prevented.","The plateau is long."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Why the AP & Refractory Period Are Long","lv":"Hard","s":"Two neighbouring ventricular regions recover at different times. An early impulse reaches them. Why can a re-entrant arrhythmia start?","c":["Equal refractory periods everywhere","Unequal refractory periods","Loss of the plateau","Heart tetany","Fast Na⁺ upstroke"],"a":1,"e":"Flip side: heterogeneous refractory periods make re-entrant arrhythmias possible when an impulse arrives at the wrong time.","w":["Uniform recovery protects against re-entry.","","Not the mechanism named.","Cardiac muscle cannot tetanize.","The upstroke is not the problem named."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's","s":"Questions 3–4<br>Correlate the ionic mechanism to the appropriate phase (labeled A–E)<br><br>3. High Na⁺ conductance","html":true,"img":"cp-fig1.svg","ns":true,"sampath":1,"c":["Phase labeled A","Phase labeled B","Phase labeled C","Phase labeled D","Phase labeled E"],"a":0,"e":"A is Phase 0, the rapid upstroke: fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open and Na⁺ rushes in.","w":["A is the Phase 0 upstroke.","B is the peak and Phase 1 notch.","C is the Phase 2 plateau.","D is Phase 3 repolarization.","E is the Phase 4 resting potential."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's","s":"Questions 3–4<br>Correlate the ionic mechanism to the appropriate phase (labeled A–E)<br><br>4. High Ca²⁺ conductance","html":true,"img":"cp-fig1.svg","ns":true,"sampath":1,"c":["Phase labeled A","Phase labeled B","Phase labeled C","Phase labeled D","Phase labeled E"],"a":2,"e":"C is Phase 2, the plateau: L-type Ca²⁺ channels are open and Ca²⁺ influx balances K⁺ efflux.","w":["A is the Phase 0 upstroke.","B is the peak and Phase 1 notch.","C is the Phase 2 plateau.","D is Phase 3 repolarization.","E is the Phase 4 resting potential."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's","s":"Is it an AP of a slow or a fast fiber?","html":true,"img":"cp-fig1.svg","ns":true,"sampath":1,"c":["Slow fiber, an SA nodal cell","Slow fiber, an AV nodal cell","Fast fiber, a ventricular myocyte","Fast fiber, a motor neuron","Fast fiber, a skeletal muscle cell"],"a":2,"e":"It is a fast fiber: a stable rest near −80 to −90 mV, a steep Phase 0 upstroke, a notch and a long plateau. That is the fast response AP of a ventricular myocyte.","w":["An SA nodal cell has no stable rest and no plateau.","An AV nodal cell has no stable rest and no plateau.","","A neuron AP lasts ~1 ms with no plateau.","A skeletal AP lasts ~10 ms with no plateau."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's","s":"What criterion you should use to distinguish between fast and slow fibers?","html":true,"img":"cp-fig1.svg","ns":true,"sampath":1,"c":["The height of the overshoot peak","The ion that carries the Phase 0 upstroke","The number of gap junctions per cell","The value of the K⁺ equilibrium potential","The amount of Ca²⁺ stored in the SR"],"a":1,"e":"Fast = Na⁺ upstroke · Slow = Ca²⁺ upstroke. A steep Na⁺-driven Phase 0 marks a fast fiber; a slow Ca²⁺-driven upstroke marks a slow fiber.","w":["Overshoot height is not the defining criterion.","","Gap junctions are not the criterion used.","EK is similar in both.","SR stores are not the criterion used."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's","s":"Explain the function of each ionic current. Know when it is turned on and off.<br><br>Which current is turned on only briefly at the very start of the AP and produces the upstroke?","html":true,"img":"cp-fig2.svg","ns":true,"sampath":1,"c":["INa","ICa","IK1","IK","ITO"],"a":0,"e":"INa is a large, very brief inward spike at time 0 — the fast Na⁺ current of Phase 0. It then turns off as the channels inactivate.","w":["","ICa turns on after the upstroke and lasts through the plateau.","IK1 is the resting current.","IK rises slowly during the plateau.","ITO is a brief outward current right after the upstroke."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's","s":"Explain the function of each ionic current. Know when it is turned on and off.<br><br>Which inward current turns on after the upstroke and slowly decays during the plateau?","html":true,"img":"cp-fig2.svg","ns":true,"sampath":1,"c":["INa","ICa","IK1","IK","ITO"],"a":1,"e":"ICa is the L-type Ca²⁺ current: it turns on after Phase 0 and slowly decays through the plateau, triggering SR Ca²⁺ release.","w":["INa lasts only a few ms.","","IK1 is outward and resting.","IK is outward.","ITO is outward and brief."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's","s":"Explain the function of each ionic current. Know when it is turned on and off.<br><br>Which current is on at rest, turns off during the plateau, and turns back on at the end of repolarization?","html":true,"img":"cp-fig2.svg","ns":true,"sampath":1,"c":["INa","ICa","IK1","IK","ITO"],"a":2,"e":"IK1, the inward rectifier, holds the resting potential. It shuts off when the cell depolarizes and turns back on during late Phase 3, helping finish repolarization.","w":["INa is off at rest.","ICa is off at rest.","","IK is off at rest and rises during the plateau.","ITO is off at rest."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's","s":"Explain the function of each ionic current. Know when it is turned on and off.<br><br>Which outward current slowly increases during the plateau and peaks as Phase 3 begins?","html":true,"img":"cp-fig2.svg","ns":true,"sampath":1,"c":["INa","ICa","IK1","IK","ITO"],"a":3,"e":"IK, the delayed rectifier, slowly builds during the plateau and drives the massive K⁺ efflux of Phase 3, then turns off at rest.","w":["INa is inward and brief.","ICa is inward.","IK1 is off during the plateau.","","ITO is brief and early."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's","s":"Explain the function of each ionic current. Know when it is turned on and off.<br><br>Which outward current turns on briefly just after the upstroke and makes the notch?","html":true,"img":"cp-fig2.svg","ns":true,"sampath":1,"c":["INa","ICa","IK1","IK","ITO"],"a":4,"e":"ITO, the transient outward K⁺ current, turns on briefly at the start of the AP and produces the Phase 1 notch, then turns off.","w":["INa is inward.","ICa is inward.","IK1 is off during the AP.","IK is slow, not transient.",""],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's","s":"Which current can be blocked by local anesthetic antiarrhythmic drugs (i.e., lidocaine)?","html":true,"img":"cp-fig2.svg","ns":true,"sampath":1,"c":["INa","ICa","IK1","IK","ITO"],"a":0,"e":"Lidocaine, a local anesthetic antiarrhythmic, blocks the fast Na⁺ current, INa, which carries the Phase 0 upstroke.","w":["","Ca²⁺ channels are blocked by drugs such as verapamil.","Not the lidocaine target.","Not the lidocaine target.","Not the lidocaine target."],"src":"lec"},{"t":"Challenge Yourself · Sampath's Q's","s":"What are the effects of Ca++ blockers on the heart and blood vessels?","html":true,"img":"cp-fig2.svg","ns":true,"sampath":1,"c":["More force and vasoconstriction","Less force and vasodilation","More force and vasodilation","Less force and vasoconstriction","No change in force or tone"],"a":1,"e":"Ca²⁺ blockers reduce L-type Ca²⁺ influx. In the heart this weakens contraction, since less trigger Ca²⁺ means less SR Ca²⁺ release; they also slow SA and AV nodal cells, whose upstroke is Ca²⁺ carried. In blood vessels they relax smooth muscle, causing vasodilation.","w":["Less Ca²⁺ means less force, and vessels relax.","","Less Ca²⁺ means less, not more, force.","Vascular smooth muscle relaxes.","Both heart and vessels respond."],"src":"lec"}];
